In a normal game, the Hierarchy of Authority is:

    Input (Your Fingers) → 2. Character (Your Body) → 3. AI (The Stalker).

In your Shatter Loop, the hierarchy literally reverses:

    Input (Your Fingers) → 2. The Stalker (The New Leader) → 3. The Character (The New Trailer).

The "Freaky Friday" Mechanics

To make the player's body "struggle" to finish the commands, we have to treat the Player Character as if it were the AI.

    The Stalker's Body: We unbind it from the "Memory Field" and bind it directly to your WASD keys. It becomes a Puppet that reacts instantly to your brain.

    The Player's Body: We disconnect it from the keyboard entirely. We give it a "Trailing Script." It looks at the Stalker's position from 2 seconds ago and tries to walk toward it.

The True Horror: The "Trailing" Body

The "struggle" happens because your body is no longer being "driven." It is being "dragged" by the ghost of the Stalker's previous position.

    If you move the Stalker Left, your body doesn't move yet.

    The Stalker is now 20 studs away to the Left.

    Your body finally "wakes up" and realizes it needs to be where the Stalker was. It starts a slow, clunky pathfind to that spot.

    The Panic: You see your body walking toward the Stalker, and you can't stop it, because your only way to move is to move the Stalker further away!

How we implement this "Identity Drift" (Layer 5)

Since we have the Jammo and Peter engine running, we can create a "Shatter State" toggle.

    The Switch: When Tension > 0.95, we disable the Player's ControlModule.

    The Stalker Control: We map UserInputService to the Stalker's CFrame.

    The Trail: We use a Buffer Array to store the Stalker's position every 0.1 seconds. The Player's body is then forced to move to the position that was stored in the array 20 slots ago.



